What became what (the translations are derivative works of these files,
each marked as changed in its header, as the license asks):
  sha256blockSha_amd64.s   -> cxx/src/sha256_shani.S  (blockSha, the one
                              sha256.go dispatches to; blockIntelSha, the
                              other SHA-NI version, is unused there and here)
  sha256blockAvx2_amd64.s  -> cxx/src/sha256_avx2.S
  sha256blockAvx_amd64.s   -> cxx/src/sha256_avx.S
  sha256blockSsse_amd64.s  -> cxx/src/sha256_ssse3.S
  sha256block_noasm.go     -> cxx/src/sha256.cpp: blocks_generic (blockGeneric)
  sha256.go                -> cxx/src/sha256.cpp: the choice of the block
                              function (init, block), the padding (checkSum)
  cpuid.go, cpuid_amd64.s  -> cxx/src/sha256.cpp: detect() (cpuid, xgetbv)
  sha256block*_amd64.go    -> the functions' arguments (documented in the .S)
The AVX-512 multi-buffer server (16 independent hashes at once), the ARM64
code and the Go API are not ported: one stream is hashed at a time.

Local changes:
  - The Go (Plan 9) assembly is written in GNU as's Intel syntax: the same
    instructions, byte for byte. The instructions the Go files give as
    encoded bytes (BYTE/WORD/LONG, with the mnemonic in a comment) are
    written as their mnemonics; the macros are .macro's. Verified: the
    objects of the four .S files were disassembled against the Go
    assembler's objects of the originals (go1.24.7 `go tool asm`): the code
    is identical but for the entries and exits below (one register move of
    the AVX2 code takes GNU as's `{load}` to keep the original's encoding).
  - The entries and exits: the System V calling convention (arguments in
    rdi, rsi, rdx) instead of Go's frame of arguments, and the callee-saved
    registers the code uses saved; the AVX and SSSE3 code keeps its scratch
    where Go's frame was (its 4 reserved arguments), in 80 bytes of stack.
    Symbols: pan_sha256_blocks_shani, _avx2, _avx, _ssse3 (hidden), with
    the CET marking (endbr64, the property note) of cxx/src/sha256_asm.h.
    The functions are declared sysv_abi in sha256.cpp, for the MinGW port.
  - The AVX2 code is chosen only where BMI1 and BMI2 are there too (it uses
    andn and rorx; sha256-simd checks AVX2 alone).
  - Tests (cxx/tests/sha256_test.cpp): the NIST CAVP vectors (ShortMsg,
    part of LongMsg, Monte Carlo) and FIPS 180-2's examples through every
    implementation the processor has, and random messages in random pieces
    against the generic one. The SHA-NI code was run under
    `qemu-x86_64 -cpu max` (QEMU 8.2), the machine having none.
